    "captions": [
      {
        "t": 0,
        "text": "Dawn, 3 July 1187. Saladin is besieging Tiberias. King Guy marches the whole army of the kingdom, about 20,000 men, from the spring of Turan towards the lake, 25 km away across dry hills."
      },
      {
        "t": 4,
        "text": "Saladin's horse archers harass the column all day, especially the rearguard of Templars and Hospitallers, which falls behind. The heat is fierce and there is no water on the plateau."
      },
      {
        "t": 9,
        "text": "Afternoon. Saladin's centre blocks the road to Tiberias. Raymond of Tripoli urges a turn north-east to the springs of Hattin, but the army halts for the night near Lubiya."
      },
      {
        "t": 12,
        "text": "Night. Saladin's army surrounds the camp. The Franks, without water, hear the Muslim soldiers praying and singing all night. Arrows are brought up by camel."
      },
      {
        "t": 22.5,
        "text": "Dawn, 4 July. Saladin's men set fire to the dry grass upwind, and smoke blows into the Franks' faces. The army tries to break through to the springs of Hattin."
      },
      {
        "t": 27,
        "text": "Raymond's knights charge Taqi al-Din's wing. It opens its ranks, lets them through, then closes again. Raymond rides away down to the lake and escapes."
      },
      {
        "t": 29,
        "text": "The foot soldiers break and climb the Horns of Hattin. The knights fight on round the king's red tent. Twice they charge towards Saladin, and twice they are driven back."
      },
      {
        "t": 33.5,
        "text": "The True Cross is captured and the king's tent falls. Saladin's son al-Afdal later remembered his father dismounting to thank God."
      },
      {
        "t": 35,
        "text": "King Guy is spared and given iced water. Reynald of Chatillon is killed by Saladin himself. About 200 Templars and Hospitallers are executed two days later. The kingdom has no army left."
      }
    ]
